def _query_value(name: str) -> str:
    value = st.query_params.get(name, "")
    if isinstance(value, list):
        value = value[0] if value else ""
    return str(value).strip().lower()


def _pilot_to_scale_article() -> str:
    return f'''{THINKING_CSS}{nav("thinking")}<main><section class="article-hero"><div class="container"><a class="article-back" href="?page=thinking" target="_self">← Back to Thinking</a><div class="article-meta">Point of view · Enterprise AI</div><h1>Why Enterprise AI Often Stalls Between Pilot and Scale</h1><p class="standfirst">A pilot can prove that an AI idea works. It does not prove that the organization is ready to own it, govern it, adopt it or repeat the result at scale.</p><div class="article-date">17 Sep 2026 · 6 min read</div></div></section><section class="section paper"><div class="container article-layout"><article class="article-body"><p>Most enterprise AI programs do not struggle because the first use case is impossible to build. The harder moment comes after something works. A team has a promising pilot, stakeholders have seen a demo, the technology has shown enough potential to create momentum — and then the question changes.</p><p>Can this become part of the way the organization actually operates?</p><p>That is a different test. The pilot tested possibility. Scale tests ownership, evidence, data, controls, workflow change and the ability to make the same kind of decision repeatedly without depending on the original project team.</p><blockquote>Technical success is evidence of possibility. Enterprise scale is evidence of operating capability.</blockquote><h2>The gap is usually organizational before it is technical</h2><p>Across large AI portfolios, I have repeatedly seen the same pattern: teams spend enormous energy proving that a model, assistant or automation can work, then discover that the operating questions were postponed. Who owns the outcome? What evidence is enough to justify broader use? Which data needs a named steward? What happens when the solution behaves differently in production? Who changes the workflow around it?</p><p>Those questions can feel secondary during experimentation because the objective is learning. At scale they become the work.</p><p>This is why a technically successful pilot can still be unready for investment. A positive demo may show usefulness without showing repeatability. It may reduce effort for a small test group without proving adoption. It may use data that cannot yet be governed consistently. It may have enthusiastic sponsorship without a durable business owner.</p><h2>The trade-off: structure too early, or structure too late</h2><p>There is a real tension here. Put too much governance and operating structure around early experiments and teams lose speed before they have learned enough. Leave every question until after the pilot and the organization creates a portfolio of attractive demonstrations with no reliable path to production.</p><p>I do not think the answer is a heavier process. It is staged evidence.</p><p>Early exploration should stay light. As an initiative asks for more money, more users, more sensitive data or more influence over real decisions, the evidence expected from it should increase. The discipline is not in forcing every idea through the same gate. It is in being explicit about what must become true before the next level of commitment is justified.</p><h2>Five questions I use before calling something scale-ready</h2><p><strong>1. Is there a real business owner?</strong><br>The person accountable for the affected outcome cannot be replaced by a project sponsor, technical lead or central AI team.</p><p><strong>2. Is the value mechanism credible?</strong><br>We should be able to explain which decision, workflow or behavior changes, how adoption will be observed and how that change connects to a business outcome.</p><p><strong>3. Can the data be owned at production standard?</strong><br>Access for a pilot is not the same as reliable ownership, semantics, quality and monitoring over time.</p><p><strong>4. Are controls proportionate to the real risk?</strong><br>The organization needs enough evidence, escalation and human oversight for the consequences of the use case — not generic controls copied from a different risk profile.</p><p><strong>5. Does the operating model survive the project team?</strong><br>If the use case only works while the original enthusiasts are present, the organization has not scaled a capability. It has extended a pilot.</p><h2>The CoE should make this path easier, not own everything</h2><p>A central AI or Data team has an important role in creating common methods, patterns, guardrails and specialist support. The failure mode is becoming the permanent owner of every AI initiative. That can accelerate the first wave and quietly create a bottleneck for the second.</p><p>My preference is a federated model with explicit thresholds: central expertise where common capability or higher risk warrants it; business ownership where value and workflow accountability belong; and a lifecycle that makes the hand-offs visible.</p><p>The goal is not to make every use case pass through more committees. It is to make the route from idea to responsible scale understandable before teams reach the expensive part.</p><div class="rule"><strong>My rule: do not scale the demo. Scale the operating capability around it.</strong><p>When ownership, evidence, data accountability, controls and adoption are explicit, technology has somewhere durable to live. Without them, another technically successful pilot rarely changes the enterprise.</p></div></article><aside class="article-aside"><span>Scale-readiness check</span><strong>Five questions before the next investment decision.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div>Who owns the business outcome?</div><div>What value mechanism are we testing?</div><div>Who owns production data quality and semantics?</div><div>What evidence and controls match the risk?</div><div>Who operates this after the project team leaves?</div></div><p>This is the practical distinction I make between proving an AI use case and building an enterprise capability.</p></aside></div></section>{opportunity()}</main>{footer()}'''


def _governance_gate_note() -> str:
    return f'''{THINKING_CSS}{nav("thinking")}<main><section class="article-hero"><div class="container"><a class="article-back" href="?page=thinking" target="_self">← Back to Thinking</a><div class="article-meta">Decision note · AI Governance</div><h1>The AI governance gate I would never remove.</h1><p class="standfirst">Explicit human accountability before an AI use case moves into production-scale decisions.</p><div class="article-date">17 Sep 2026 · 1 min read</div></div></section><section class="section paper"><div class="container"><article class="note-full"><h2>Governance can be lighter. Accountability cannot disappear.</h2><p>A risk review can be simplified, automated or tiered. Documentation can change with the level of exposure. But before an AI use case influences a real business decision at scale, I want one thing to be unambiguous: a human owner for the outcome. Someone must know what the system is meant to influence, what evidence makes its use acceptable, what should be monitored and when the organization should stop or intervene. My rule is simple: if nobody can answer “who is accountable when this changes a real decision?”, the use case is not ready to scale.</p></article></div></section>{opportunity()}</main>{footer()}'''


def _thinking_landing() -> str:
    return f'''{THINKING_CSS}{nav("thinking")}<main><section class="pagehero"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Thinking</p><h1>Current thinking on the decisions behind enterprise AI.</h1><p>I use this page as a working record of the operating questions I keep encountering: what deserves investment, what is ready to scale, where governance helps or gets in the way, how ownership should work and what turns AI activity into durable business capability.</p></div></section><section class="section paper"><div class="container"><div class="head"><div><p class="eyebrow">What I’m thinking about now</p><h2>Fresh arguments, short notes and practical frameworks.</h2></div><p>New material appears here first. The purpose is not publishing volume; it is to make the judgment behind enterprise AI and Data leadership visible while the questions are still current.</p></div><div class="thinking-now"><article class="featured-thinking"><div class="kicker">Week 1 · Point of view</div><h2>Why Enterprise AI Often Stalls Between Pilot and Scale</h2><p>A technically successful pilot proves possibility. Scale asks a harder set of questions about ownership, evidence, data, controls and adoption — and that is where many AI portfolios lose momentum.</p><a class="read-live" href="?page=thinking&amp;article=pilot-to-scale" target="_self" data-hq-event="thinking_article_pilot_scale_open">Read the full piece →</a><div class="meta">17 Sep 2026 · Enterprise AI · 6 min</div></article><article class="decision-note"><div class="kicker">Week 1 · Decision note</div><h3>The AI governance gate I would never remove.</h3><p>Before an AI use case influences real business decisions at scale, there must be an explicit human owner for the outcome. Controls can be tiered. Accountability cannot be implicit.</p><a class="read-live" style="margin-top:18px;font-size:11px;font-weight:850;text-decoration:none" href="?page=thinking&amp;article=governance-accountability" target="_self" data-hq-event="thinking_note_governance_open">Read note →</a><div class="meta">17 Sep 2026 · AI Governance · 1 min</div></article></div><div class="format-strip"><div class="format-card"><span>Point of view</span><strong>A position worth defending</strong><p>Longer arguments on enterprise AI decisions, trade-offs and operating choices.</p></div><div class="format-card"><span>Field notes</span><strong>What practice teaches</strong><p>Observations shaped by recurring patterns in enterprise work.</p></div><div class="format-card"><span>Decision notes</span><strong>One judgment, briefly</strong><p>Short positions on a specific leadership or governance decision.</p></div><div class="format-card"><span>Frameworks</span><strong>Reusable ways to think</strong><p>Simple models for portfolio, operating-model, adoption and value questions.</p></div></div><div class="themebar"><span>AI Strategy</span><span>Operating Models</span><span>AI Governance</span><span>Adoption</span><span>Portfolio &amp; Value</span><span>Data &amp; Analytics Leadership</span></div></div></section><section class="section white"><div class="container"><div class="head"><div><p class="eyebrow">Leading in the AI Enterprise</p><h2>Selected essays from the longer series.</h2></div><p>The archive provides deeper context around strategy, value, operating models, governance, data readiness and adoption. The newer material above is where the page stays current.</p></div>{thought_grid(THOUGHTS,"articles")}<div class="actions"><a class="btn dark" href="{MEDIUM}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-hq-event="medium_thinking">More writing on Medium ↗</a><a class="btn dark" href="{LINKEDIN}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-hq-event="linkedin_thinking">LinkedIn ↗</a></div></div></section><section class="section paper"><div class="container editorial-split"><div><p class="eyebrow">How I use writing</p><h2>A point of view is useful only when it improves a decision.</h2><p>I write selectively, usually when a recurring operating question is worth working through. The strongest pieces start from tensions I have seen in enterprise work and force me to take a position I would be willing to defend with a leadership team.</p><div class="grid3" style="margin-top:24px"><article class="card"><span class="org">Experience first</span><h3>Start from a real operating tension</h3><p>Governance versus speed, experimentation versus scale, centralized expertise versus business responsibility, or technical possibility versus adoption. These tensions produce better thinking than trend summaries.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="org">Judgment</span><h3>State the trade-off clearly</h3><p>Most enterprise decisions are not solved by a universal best practice. I try to explain what changes the answer, what information matters and which compromise I would choose in a given context.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="org">Practical use</span><h3>Leave the reader with a better question</h3><p>The useful test is whether a piece helps someone frame a problem or decision more clearly, challenge an assumption or make the next choice with more precision.</p></article></div></div><div class="editorial-photo portrait"><img src="{THINKING_PANEL_URI}" alt="Jair Ribeiro listening during an industry panel discussion" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div></section><section class="section white"><div class="container speak"><img src="{PANEL_DIALOGUE_URI}" alt="Jair Ribeiro contributing to a conference panel discussion" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><div><p class="eyebrow">External context</p><h2>Writing, speaking and research extend the operating perspective.</h2><p>The same questions appear in practice, research and executive conversations. Writing and speaking give me another way to test whether an argument remains useful outside the context in which it was formed.</p><div class="prooflist"><div class="proofitem"><strong>Thinkers360</strong> — Top 50 Global Thought Leaders &amp; Influencers on Emerging Technology (2023).</div><div class="proofitem"><strong>Generative AI Summit 2023, London</strong> — keynote on scaling AI adoption across the enterprise.</div><div class="proofitem"><strong>Research focus</strong> — MSc work on Responsible AI adoption in global enterprises.</div></div></div></div></section>{opportunity()}</main>{footer()}'''


def thinking() -> str:
    article = _query_value("article")
    if article == "pilot-to-scale":
        return _pilot_to_scale_article()
    if article == "governance-accountability":
        return _governance_gate_note()
    return _thinking_landing()
